  DEFENDING ARMY

  The attackers had the 210th Camel Corps, the 305th Light Cavalry and the 
306th Light Cavalry in reserve.  Bradipo I led his armies into the fray.  No 
generals were present to lead the forces of the provincial Eagle chieftain.
  Bradipo I had used a long flanking march to try to suprise the provincial 
Eagle chieftain.  But the ruse was detected, and its only effect was to tire 
Bradipo I's troops considerably.  But Bradipo I deployed his troops against 
the provincial Eagle chieftain with excellent tactical foresight and 
obtained a tremendous tactical superiority.

  On the left flank, the Crack 33rd Legion were led into battle by a troop 
of the Order of Knights Hospitaller, who inspired them to fanaticism with 
their Good and vaunted leadership.  They were arrayed against the 18th 
Eagles.  The 18th Eagles soared over the 33rd Legion and attacked them.  The 
16th Eagles soared over the 289th Phalanx and attacked them.  On this 
battleground, the spirits of the soldiers of the 33rd Legion were raised 
when one sergeant shouted "Long live the King!"  , and the rallying cry was 
taken up by all of the 33rd Legion.  The 33rd Legion battled with the 18th 
Eagles and stout-heartedly advanced against them.
  On the right wing, the Crack 31st Legion faced the 17th Eagles.  The 17th 
Eagles soared over the 31st Legion and attacked them.  The 19th Eagles 
soared over the 265th Axemen and attacked without mercy.  In this area, some 
men of the 31st Legion had been drawn off by spurious reports of a battle 
elsewhere, and entered battle with a hefty disadvantage.  Still, the 31st 
Legion fought the 17th Eagles and moved ahead slowly, contested for every 
yard of their advance.
  Bradipo I's armies vanquished the army opposing them with a final 
determined charge.  The 33rd Legion destroyed the 18th Eagles.  The 289th 
Phalanx slew the 16th Eagles almost to a man.
  Kiska was overrun by Bradipo I's men in a bloody campaign.  The battle 
affected the quality of our forces:  the mayhem wreaked by the 306th 
Light Cavalry during the battle has raised the spirits of its warriors, and 
all agree that it has risen to Veteran quality.  The 17th Eagles and the 
19th Eagles were unable to retreat and were paroled into the population.
  In early summer, Bradipo I practiced his Necromancy diligently for months, 
but could make no headway.
  With the coming of the late summer season Bradipo I practiced his Tactics 
well, and even consulted a palm reader, but could not improve his skill in 
Tactics.
  The hot days of summer mellowed into the fall.  Bradipo I recruited enough 
men in Kiska to form the 207th Eagles for the army of Bradipo I.
  During early winter, Bradipo I attempted an illegal move.  He was ordered 
to move from Kiska to Jadegate, further than his army could move.  Instead, 
he worked hard on his Tactics but was thwarted in his self-improvement 
efforts.

  Thus endeth the adventures of Bradipo I for this year.


  Bradipo I treated with his ally Kwoth (#3).  Bradipo I treated with his 
ally Hokuto (#4).  Bradipo I treated with his ally Prometeo (#5).  Bradipo I 
treated with his ally Kahn (#6).  Bradipo I treated with his ally 
Giovanna d'Arco (#7).  Bradipo I treated with his ally Circe (#8).  
Bradipo I treated with his ally Agesilao (#9).
  Your Majesty, I have news to report of wanderers in our kingdom:  
  Drago (#39) grew tired of the scenery in Orielle and left for Chaco.
  Brunhilda (#86) left Abu Matariq to preside over a reunion of her old 
battalion in Mongu.
  The 206th Trolls dissolved their company and quit the mercenary life.


  Here is the financial report for Bradipo I's kingdom: this turn, the 
Kingdom collected 41 gold in revenue.  2 gold was expended to finance our 
leaders.  The 207th Eagles trained well and improved in quality.  24 gold 
was expended to maintain our armies.  Bradipo I was left with 54 gold in the 
treasury.


LETTER REPORTS

  O King of Kings, thy heroes have travelled around the globe in
search of adventure and conquest.  I have faithfully kept their
correspondence, and now I present it to thee:


From: Adele
To: Bradipo I

  Honored Sovereign, 

  Frigid winter melted into spring, and I made ready to carry out my urgent 
orders.  I moved into Mongu.  I saw no monsters.
  During early summer, I really tried hard to improve my Element Magic, but 
I practiced in vain.
  With the coming of the late summer season, I could not cast Flood on 
Transylvania because Mountain terrain is illegal for the Flood spell.  So, I 
practiced my Exploring for many weeks, but could make no significant 
headway.
  In fall, I took a deep, nervous draught from my bottle of scotch, and 
approached the Cave.  I encountered the Renegade Swordsman.  Incanting a 
Hand Of Death spell, I channelled scintillating, writhing strands of magic 
and began making squeezing motions with my hands.
  The Renegade Swordsman moved to bow range.
  The Renegade Swordsman closed to melee range, filled with maniacal 
blood-lust.  I seized his spine with phantasmal hands and shook it and 
damaged the Renegade Swordsman, inflicting 2 damage points.  
The Renegade Swordsman arrogantly thrust at me and scored on me, inflicting 5
points of damage.  My spell was disrupted by the event.  Lifting my hands 
high in the air, I cast a Fireball spell and scored 8 damage points on 
the Renegade Swordsman.
  The Renegade Swordsman whirled about and swang at me but I dodged the 
sword.  I could not cast a Ice Suspension spell because I had already cast 
my allowed number of Element Magic spells.  The Renegade Swordsman swang at 
me with all his might but I dodged the blow.  I thrust at 
the Renegade Swordsman and missed.  The Renegade Swordsman swang at me and 
inflicted 4 damage points.  I deftly thrust at the Renegade Swordsman and 
walloped the Renegade Swordsman with 1 damage points.
  I fled unceremoniously from the scene.
  I saw nothing special while scouting out the Cave.  During my travels, I 
was stopped by a local band of militia.  Their commanding captain 
threateningly challenged me.  But when I was taken to the local sheriff, I 
showed him the official seal of Bradipo I, and was let go.

  The fall cooled into early winter.  I took a nervous gulp from my bottle 
of scotch, and entered the Crypt.  I encountered the Gargoyle.  I thought to 
attempt a Hand Of Death spell, but dropped a crucial material component 
before I could make my attempt.
  The Gargoyle moved into bow range.  I chanted my mysterious words of magic 
and cast a Fireball spell and damaged the Gargoyle, inflicting 6 damage 
points.
  The Gargoyle closed to melee range, filled with blood-lust.  The Gargoyle 
swang at me and scored on me, doing 4 points of damage.  I could not cast a 
Ice Suspension spell because I had already cast my allowed number of Element 
Magic spells.  I whirled around and audaciously thrust at the Gargoyle and 
scored 8 damage points on the Gargoyle.  The Gargoyle pretended to panic, 
then pivoted and treacherously slashed at me and inflicted 1 damage points.  
The Gargoyle forced away me with its aggressive assault.  The Gargoyle then 
slashed at me with maximum force and scorched me with 1 damage points.  I 
thrust at my enemy and damaged the Gargoyle, scoring 7 damage points.  
The Gargoyle staggered, stunned by the force of the mortal blow.  I shifted 
my attention to the treasure of the Gargoyle.  I bagged the Large Hoard of 
Gold.  I looted 20 gold from the treasure of the Gargoyle.
  While scouting the Crypt, I saw a Book which seemed to be of a magical 
nature.
  Magic runes formed on the Book, saying "The Book gives you the magic power 
to cast a Part Sea spell.  The "Part Sea" will take effect on this province 
(for a land province, it can be used to cancel a Flood spell.)  You must pay 2
gold each time the Book is used (to show that you accept these terms, a 
character must give two Scout orders when he Searches this feature.)"  While 
adventuring, I saw no monsters.

  Thy humble servant,   Adele


From: Indra
To: Bradipo I

  Honored Sovereign, 

  In spring, I discovered nothing worthy of note in my sagely researches.
  The wet spring blossomed into early summer.  I invented nothing in my 
sagely researches.
  With the coming of the late summer season I discovered nothing worthy of 
note in my sagely researches.
  In fall, I discovered nothing in my sagely researches.
  While speaking to some farmers, I heard a rumor about a Cave in Katanga 
(#404), inhabited by the Succubus.  They maintain that the Succubus actually 
owns the Necronomicon.
  The fall season cooled into early winter.  I invented nothing valuable in 
my sagely experiments.

  Thy humble servant,   Indra


